#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1500.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097486} :activity/id #uuid "90bcccea-5782-59d1-a524-d6852abe26cd" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048780} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097486 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097837 :tutorial/content "Given a fixed **origin** in the plane (often denoted $O$), and another point $X,$ the vector $\\overrightarrow{OX}$ is called the **position vector** of the point $X.$\n\nSo, the position vector tells us how to move from the origin to the point.\n\nFor example, suppose $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\overrightarrow{A X} = \\mathbf{b} .$ How can we find the position vector $\\overrightarrow{OX}$ in terms of $\\mathbf a$ and $\\mathbf{b}?$\n\n![](images/e6/e61003fb62534b04cfbbac07a64e61f6c16e2d464eb35bc5b98654b0603b24db.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we can trace a path from $O$ to $X$ by first moving from $O$ to $A,$ and then from $A$ to $X.$ Therefore,\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OX} = \\overrightarrow{OA}+\\overrightarrow{AX}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\overrightarrow{A X} = \\mathbf{b} ,$ we get\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{O X} = \\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b} .\n$$\n\nSo, to find a position vector, we can trace a convenient path from the origin to the point and add the vectors along that path." :tutorial/id #uuid "055562c0-b02b-58fe-93a0-5e216241c04f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2910 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e10b4911-140a-5838-a16e-13feb12fba7b" :step/math-academy-id 661397 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362445}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097487 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096652 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096451} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b6a076-8795-5510-aabc-62a925826dd0" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735264} :knowledge-point/questions [{