:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256582} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256611} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256624} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294966} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302111} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302112} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302113} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302114} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302115} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302116} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302117} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302118} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302119} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329678} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387001} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390558} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420465} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514792} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514793} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514814} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514819} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514820} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514826} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514830} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514835} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514836} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186582357} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186734987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186734990} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186734994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735290} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735291} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735294} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735298} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735301}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector That Satisfies a Ratio in Terms of Known Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "eb2bab86-218f-5b3a-9a4e-b0b3872ba6db" :step/math-academy-id 661411} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097564 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096701 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096495} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fbb8f19-f35a-59c4-9b77-8f6c3542ef28" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050467} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051014}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735401} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097164} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292806} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302041} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302311} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338753} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338754} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338756} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338757} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420466} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572666} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572695} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572704} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735030} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735040} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735413} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735416} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735419} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735423}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor in a Linear Combination"} :step/id #uuid "74a3f281-5081-51cb-a589-0b5531ea612f" :step/math-academy-id 661399 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362452}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362445 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362404 :tutorial/content "Now let's look at a slightly more complicated diagram.\n\nSuppose we have two vectors $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf{b},$ and a parallelogram $OAXB_1,$ where $\\overrightarrow{O {B}_{1}} = 2 \\overrightarrow{O B} .$ How can we find the position vector $\\mathbf{x} = \\overrightarrow{O X}$ in terms of $\\mathbf a$ and $\\mathbf{b}?$\n\n![](images/c7/c78c8895790b28c7dbe60d0dd31a5375fef19ba2d6fa668144859e6fafaf33bb.png)\n\nAs before, we trace a path from the origin to the point $X.$ From the diagram, we can write\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = \\overrightarrow{O {B}_{1}} + \\overrightarrow{{B}_{1} X} .\n$$\n\nWe know that\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{O {B}_{1}} = 2 \\overrightarrow{O B} = 2 \\mathbf{b} .\n$$\n\nAlso, since $OAXB_1$ is a parallelogram, opposite sides are equal and parallel. So,\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{B_1X}=\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf{a}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the expression for $\\mathbf x,$ we get\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc}\\mathbf{x} &  = \\overrightarrow{O {B}_{1}} + \\overrightarrow{{B}_{1} X} \\\\  &  = 2 \\mathbf{b} + \\mathbf{a} \\\\  &  = \\mathbf{a} + 2 \\mathbf{b} .\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo, even when the path uses translated copies of known vectors, we can still find the position vector by expressing each part of the path in terms of the known vectors and then adding.\n\nNow we will use the same path-tracing idea in a slightly different way. Instead of only writing a position vector, we will also use vector paths to identify unknown coefficients in linear combinations of known vectors." :tutorial/id #uuid "bd7a9db3-e422-5170-83a1-857ca34b9bc8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 15988 :tutorial/title "Writing a Position Vector Using Known Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "57d87818-8242-532e-8177-977c7fa7906e" :step/math-academy-id 661398 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097564}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362452 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362403 :tutorial/content "We can still find a position vector by tracing a path from the origin to the point and adding the vectors along that path.\n\nHowever, if one part of the path points in the opposite direction to a known vector, then we write it as a **negative** multiple of that vector.\n\nFor example, consider the picture below. \n\n![](images/d5/d5366a68ed98e7f614ff998f69098613ea8e12d05bc27274ea66e8ce5a908cb7.png)\n\nWe are given that $\\mathbf{c} = \\lambda  \\mathbf{a} + 2 \\mathbf{b}$ and we are asked to find $\\lambda.$\n\nTo find $\\lambda,$ we want to match the expression\n$$\n\\mathbf{c} = \\lambda  \\mathbf{a} + 2 \\mathbf{b} .\n$$\n\nLet $O$ be the origin, and let $\\mathbf a$ and $\\mathbf c$ end at points $A$ and $C,$ as shown in the diagram below.\n\nSince one term in the vector sum is $2 \\mathbf{b} ,$ we first draw the vector $2 \\mathbf{b}$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/bc/bc976b12b4610c69953545d95662bf5535ef2a92eb9d9cbd63e087c85f3b98e7.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $2 \\mathbf{b} .$ Then\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c} = \\overrightarrow{O P} + \\overrightarrow{P C} .\n$$\n\nFrom the construction, we have\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{O P} = 2 \\mathbf{b} .\n$$\n\nAlso, the vector $\\overrightarrow{P C}$ has length $2 | \\mathbf{a} | ,$ but points in the opposite direction to $\\mathbf a.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{P C} = - 2 \\mathbf{a} .\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the expression for $\\mathbf{c},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc}\\mathbf{c} &  = \\overrightarrow{O P} + \\overrightarrow{P C} \\\\  &  = 2 \\mathbf{b} + ( - 2 \\mathbf{a} ) \\\\  &  = - 2 \\mathbf{a} + 2 \\mathbf{b} .\\end{array}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n$$\n\\mathbf{c} = \\lambda  \\mathbf{a} + 2 \\mathbf{b} = - 2 \\mathbf{a} + 2 \\mathbf{b} .\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a} \\nparallel \\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf a,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\lambda=-2.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b5155eff-3506-551b-8dc0-597bff07650f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 15990 :tutorial/title "Vectors in Opposite Directions"} :step/id #uuid "d0bece94-821b-52ea-b6b1-d2373c5447e0" :step/math-academy-id 661404 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362453}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362453 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235285 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234976} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58a49d00-cc58-5228-af38-9369cf31d8c8" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735595} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235344} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235345} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235565} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235566} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235567} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235568} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235569} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235570} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235571} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235572} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235573} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235581} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235582} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515712} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515721} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515727} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515732} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515734} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735154} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735155} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735164} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735569} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735570} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735575} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735581} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186735587}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor When Direction Reverses"} :step/id #uuid "d4d86c29-1b09-51e8-ae95-b9a5b6ceb242" :step/math-academy-id 661405 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097487}}] :activity/title "Describing the Position Vector of a Point Using Known Vectors" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096886}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420468} :activity/id #uuid "bf7c0114-33c8-5bb5-b8a8-2b8fac8eb7ef" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048780} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420468 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420467 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f1fbbe3-94d8-5bd3-8727-4e6f9a832097" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\lambda =1$ and $\\mu =2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420448} {:answer/id #uuid "90599886-7bfb-5b72-84ac-b60df87c5d99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\lambda =3$ and $\\mu =2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420452} {:answer/id #uuid "a6826047-3513-5e97-9ed8-1ae795633fc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420453} {:answer/id #uuid "b05969db-3a13-5b96-96be-8b739e33d310" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420455} {:answer/id #uuid "cf620219-7b28-5e23-a450-b4abd046ba47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420457}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420453} :answer-field/id #uuid "979e9c27-4560-5e47-8337-19ce5dc8192c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420463}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d5f98ecb-0f26-50ae-9a82-cc334fd82c55" :question/math-academy-id "q-20413" :question/problem "![](images/4b/4bb7598c9ebae28be572508974cd025caa8f4a6c3313f025ff0f5ca55296334e.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $h=\\lambda f+\\mu g$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/43/43642acdb991827b4e1e16744e8a69507a383a1319ae07efde78480af4bab166.png)\n\nIn $△OFH$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OF=2|g|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OF}$ and $g$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OF}=2g.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $FH=3|f|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{FH}$ has the opposite direction to $f$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{FH}=-3f.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h & =\\overset{\\to }{OF}+\\overset{\\to }{FH} \\\\  & =2g-3f \\\\  & =-3f+2g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $h=\\lambda f+\\mu g=-3f+2g.$\n\nSince $f∦g,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =2$"} :step/id #uuid "509e4633-305b-52c6-89d7-db63ec41fd87" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420469}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420469 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420466 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "271316bb-de9b-5d8b-bad0-ffd1bfc5b101" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420447} {:answer/id #uuid "633d97e3-8d84-57c9-8ac7-bdd2378d9d50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420449} {:answer/id #uuid "c2051bdd-22cb-509c-8fb6-340e90eb858d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420456} {:answer/id #uuid "e2e4325a-2b33-5c98-a020-248e21f996f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420459} {:answer/id #uuid "e3d19e05-07c5-5adc-9a1c-131ee586eacd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420460}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420447} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6f94aaa-a8cf-5aa4-9f93-884e35c10a15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420464}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b667d36f-710b-5326-b636-9597ec1ec3f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-77230" :question/problem "![](images/8c/8cf7d0520b1d0654d5749651595acd48b7a08f2b2d55142b18ca63b2ded20f55.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+2b$, find $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+2b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $2b.$ So, we first draw the vector $2b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/9e/9edea1e3ad727d11d7bfac884f27c1b435233fa7df561dbe2a4f3af45ea16bfa.png)\n\nIn $△OBC$, we have:\n\n- $BC=3|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{BC}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{BC}=3a.$ \n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OB}+\\overset{\\to }{BC} \\\\  & =2b+3a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=2b+\\lambda a=2b+3a.$ \n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a$, we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =3\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "561c8240-56f6-5b3d-9001-524cd68b5a86" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420470}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420470 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420465 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "747da426-8469-5cfb-b9c1-0f275aa12f5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\frac{1}{4}a+\\frac{3}{4}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420450} {:answer/id #uuid "7870ed39-d3a3-5476-9037-af3a46374a41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\frac{5}{8}a+\\frac{3}{8}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420451} {:answer/id #uuid "ac24f6e2-146b-56c4-926e-7b7b203e75f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\frac{3}{8}a+\\frac{5}{8}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420454} {:answer/id #uuid "d4a4f00d-a543-5fec-976f-fd2b59d619f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\frac{3}{4}a+\\frac{1}{4}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420458} {:answer/id #uuid "f78f9d21-45cd-5cd7-95b8-055e70bef457" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\frac{7}{8}a+\\frac{1}{8}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420461}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420451} :answer-field/id #uuid "0cd7bc1d-4953-5e13-8ab6-2c158bebc612" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420462}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6fe46ce7-a162-566e-a261-116569794a12" :question/math-academy-id "q-120289" :question/problem "![](images/52/52e25ccc5b59b3eecd914c5b141e443475a5de58299404528d27f1fc85db97ea.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=a,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OB}=b$, and $T$ divides $\\overset{―}{AB}$ in the ratio $AT:TB=3:5.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OT}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AT}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=a,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{AT}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OAB,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{AO}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-a+b \\\\  & =b-a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $AT:TB=3:5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{AT}=\\frac{3}{8}\\overset{\\to }{AB}=\\frac{3}{8}(b-a).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OT} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AT} \\\\  & =a+\\frac{3}{8}(b-a) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}a+\\frac{3}{8}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6fb35957-a4df-5eb9-90fe-225f93ca7c52"}] :activity/title "Describing the Position Vector of a Point Using Known Vectors" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420446}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186582462} :activity/id #uuid "bae8f9bc-92c0-5baf-aaa0-a6a67b67c339" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048780} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186582425 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186582357 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01e04c7d-dd4a-5ae8-baa2-1c4250593dea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581854}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186581854} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0a61f88-5e65-52a0-83b9-18a026bc77b1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582322} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f03bae05-655b-59e7-b9bd-7be62003684e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582203}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186582203} :answer-field/id #uuid "f127bd55-0f8c-5a8d-bfe1-2279ad5036d0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582337}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2e2f6d0e-fbd4-52a2-bc5a-47ead1dbf4b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-322579" :question/problem "![](images/eb/eb43aa9d35e08f19fff74f030fc5246428b83f7bce907a890a3b8dfa62571dc2.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OC}=c,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OR}=r,$ and $X$ divides $\\overset{―}{CR}$ in the ratio $CX:XR=6:11.$ Express $\\overset{\\to }{OX}$ via the vectors $c$ and $r.$\n\n$\\,\\overset{\\to }{OX}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$c+$\n{{field-2}}\n$r$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OX}=\\overset{\\to }{OC}+\\overset{\\to }{CX}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OC}=c,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{CX}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OCR,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{CR} & =\\overset{\\to }{CO}+\\overset{\\to }{OR} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OC}+\\overset{\\to }{OR} \\\\  & =-c+r \\\\  & =r-c.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $CX:XR=6:11,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{CX}=\\frac{6}{17}\\overset{\\to }{CR}=\\frac{6}{17}(r-c).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OX} & =\\overset{\\to }{OC}+\\overset{\\to }{CX} \\\\  & =c+\\frac{6}{17}(r-c) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{17}c+\\frac{6}{17}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "25ff7e7c-acb7-5c9d-b8d6-bc2d0c1927a1"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186582435 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235582 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ab5b44a-6f14-5d8c-b994-0ff723092bf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234586} {:answer/id #uuid "2b5b4c72-e340-52b5-be53-6903f63361a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234611} {:answer/id #uuid "cbe36434-9519-583f-9b69-c587e4937715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234866} {:answer/id #uuid "ce1ea7c1-61ca-56ba-a7d9-91573e3d6a5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234869} {:answer/id #uuid "f46ca5b6-1fd3-597e-9657-e4197a5f9d69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234933}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234586} :answer-field/id #uuid "1aa48e98-a238-5102-8717-85e261cb4e21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234997}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "adcdd06d-1586-51df-9df4-c005428c239d" :question/math-academy-id "q-77451" :question/problem "![](images/28/28faea3a553c505a8a5ab9bc7dd6e784dd23a47c336c70d31218fbe249d57cff.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+3b,$ find $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+3b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $3b.$ So, we first draw the vector $3b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/52/52421034c2919620a0a9aae844db0eba2d396e5d6f1152d69bee40ff56c4d8ad.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $3b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=3|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-3a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =3b-3a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+3b=-3a+3b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-3\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5adcc148-c9e3-5760-b08b-6b59eadbc85e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186582441}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186582441 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186582353 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05da8884-0b84-53b4-be8d-0c4ee296a4ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581860} {:answer/id #uuid "5518a4f0-c680-5b9b-baf1-dcd20aab042e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-2$ and $\\mu =3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581971} {:answer/id #uuid "84331dd6-6bc5-518f-b9ff-e7302d0e4b1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\lambda =3$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582042} {:answer/id #uuid "9fd54c5f-bc9e-5214-9bad-257eb4fdb6e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582086} {:answer/id #uuid "a8db350d-738f-580d-959b-caceb8c956c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582103}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186582086} :answer-field/id #uuid "b33f9199-1c48-55ab-bf55-87ed20786849" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186582303}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1a51e863-8cb5-5ea3-be4b-fcc4293d84e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-120275" :question/problem "![](images/cb/cbd0ffe0fb9e879f2a93557da1aef08622c9e048e14d83c4f4619cf5a1cd47bb.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $w=\\lambda u+\\mu v$, find $\\lambda$ and $\\mu .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/fb/fba4b74fde44c862267cd2c58852f4f829f5b07ead948b0c18d79e94f7d1c0fc.png)\n\nIn $△OUW$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OU=2|u|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OU}$ and $u$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OU}=2u.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $UW=3|v|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{UW}$ has the opposite direction to $v$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{UW}=-3v.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w & =\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{UW} \\\\  & =2u-3v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $w=\\lambda u+\\mu v=2u-3v.$\n\nSince $u∦v,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =-3$"} :step/id #uuid "7a8b2a38-bdad-51ba-890d-75aa8fcea14d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186582425}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186582462 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329675 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08c24834-8cd4-5bea-b65d-b2c5663746e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327004} {:answer/id #uuid "43f90f38-cbf5-5d7a-920b-1121b5764f3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\lambda =0$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327528} {:answer/id #uuid "ab7ece4b-a049-56ac-8617-bdfeb30b3311" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328419} {:answer/id #uuid "b21ee6fb-0023-5a59-b1cc-92b08b1e5bb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\lambda =3$ and $\\mu =2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328478} {:answer/id #uuid "baf8f242-62cb-5b3c-a8e9-9e47509ff9d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328564}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327004} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce77403f-2bf1-5fb6-9d50-de0552a7684f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329549}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4f6563bc-e431-538e-8c1d-8eaa7d0e394f" :question/math-academy-id "q-42657" :question/problem "![](images/dc/dc708da9adb4f61161ee1face89403bf1a6ce9762905e8a59b4b3ed9031c817a.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/84/84f8951c4921eca20b00132996dc7b2dfafe047d58574e1822da795e1444c861.png)\n\nIn $△OPR$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OP=3|p|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ has the opposite direction to $p$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=-3p.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $PR=2|q|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ has the opposite direction to $q$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PR}=-2q.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & =-3p-2q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q=-3p-2q.$\n\nSince $p∦q,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =-2$"} :step/id #uuid "bd334286-7f33-5ae1-8b2f-9a599bd1d7a2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186582435}}] :activity/title "Describing the Position Vector of a Point Using Known Vectors" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186581850}]}
